{"id":"0af22584-c4d6-41eb-9cf4-2a4d4aba493d","object":"chat.completion","created":1784832943,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\"implementation\":\"def solve(case):\\n    import collections\\n    # Validate required fields\\n    if 'payload' not in case:\\n        raise ValueError('Missing payload')\\n    payload = case['payload']\\n    if 'nodes' not in payload or 'required_symbols' not in payload:\\n        raise ValueError('Missing nodes or required_symbols')\\n    nodes = payload['nodes']\\n    required_symbols = payload['required_symbols']\\n    if not isinstance(nodes, list) or not isinstance(required_symbols, list):\\n        raise ValueError('Invalid types')\\n    # Build node map and children\\n    node_by_id = {}\\n    children_map = collections.defaultdict(list)\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        nid = n.get('id')\\n        if nid is None:\\n            raise ValueError('Node missing id')\\n        for key in ('parent','symbol','depth','removable','utility'):\\n            if key not in n:\\n                raise ValueError(f'Node {nid} missing {key}')\\n        node_by_id[nid] = n\\n        parent = n['parent']\\n        if parent is not None:\\n            children_map[parent].append(nid)\\n    # Ensure root exists and has parent None\\n    root_id = None\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        if n['parent'] is None:\\n            if root_id is not None:\\n                raise ValueError('Multiple roots')\\n            root_id = n['id']\\n    if root_id is None:\\n        raise ValueError('No root node')\\n    # Check all required symbols are initially present\\n    initial_symbols = {n['symbol'] for n in nodes}\\n    for sym in required_symbols:\\n        if sym not in initial_symbols:\\n            raise ValueError(f'Required symbol {sym} not present')\\n    # Initial retained set: all node ids\\n    retained = set(node_by_id.keys())\\n    symbol_counts = collections.Counter(n['symbol'] for n in nodes)\\n    # Sort nodes by depth descending, then id ascending (string order)\\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(node_by_id.values(), key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\\n    removal_order = []\\n    for node in sorted_nodes:\\n        nid = node['id']\\n        if nid not in retained:\\n            continue\\n        if not node['removable']:\\n            continue\\n        # Check children: if any child is retained, cannot remove\\n        children = children_map.get(nid, [])\\n        if any(cid in retained for cid in children):\\n            continue\\n        sym = node['symbol']\\n        # If removing would cause a required symbol to vanish, skip\\n        if sym in required_symbols and symbol_counts[sym] == 1:\\n            continue\\n        # Remove\\n        retained.remove(nid)\\n        symbol_counts[sym] -= 1\\n        removal_order.append(nid)\\n    # Return sorted retained ids and removal order\\n    return {\\n        'retained_ids': sorted(retained),\\n        'removal_order': removal_order\\n    }\\n\"}","reasoning_content":"We need to output exactly one JSON object with key \"implementation\", value being Python source code for solve(case). The task is to implement grammar_guided_node_reduction. The input case has nodes with id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility. Required symbols. Need to reduce the tree by tentatively removing removable nodes, but only if all required symbols remain represented. Also must not remove root, required symbols, or nodes with retained children. Processing deeper nodes before shallower, ties broken by node ID. Return sorted retained IDs and actual removal order. The function must be deterministic, no randomness, etc.\n\nGiven public fixtures, we can design algorithm:\n\n1. Build adjacency: children list per parent id.\n2. Determine initial retained set: all nodes? Actually we need to start with all nodes? The reduction process tries to remove removable nodes subject to constraints. We'll start with all nodes, then attempt to remove nodes in order: deeper first (higher depth), then by node ID ascending? \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes.\" Then tie break by node ID.\n\nWe need to compute removal order list, and final retained IDs sorted (canonical order?). The fixture examples? Not provided expected output. But we implement as described.\n\nConstraints: cannot remove root (depth 0), not removable flag false, required symbols must be present after removal. Also cannot remove node if it has children that are retained (i.e., if any descendant is retained, the parent must be retained? Actually \"node with retained children\" means if any child is retained, the parent cannot be removed. Because removing parent would orphan children. So before removing a node, check that it has no retained children (i.e., all its children are either already removed or maybe not? The node's children are defined by parent field. For a node to be removable, it must have no children that are currently retained. That implies that we need to process in topological order? Typically bottom-up: remove leaf nodes first. But the problem says deeper nodes before shallower, which is consistent. So we iterate nodes sorted by depth descending, then by ID ascending. For each such node, if it is removable (removable flag True) and not required symbol (but required symbols list includes root? root is always required. We can check: if node.symbol in required_symbols, then cannot remove even if removable? Actually required_symbols list: \"Treat these registered properties as hard contracts: ... interestingness_preserved\" but specifically required_symbols: \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" So nodes with symbol appearing in required_symbols list cannot be removed. But note that root's symbol is \"root\", which is in required_symbols (first element). So we can just check if node.symbol in required_symbols. Also root's removable is false anyway.\n\nAdditionally, need to ensure that after removal, all required symbols are still present in the tree (i.e., among retained nodes). Since we only remove a node if its symbol is not required? Actually if we remove a node that has a symbol that is required, that would remove that symbol. So we must not remove any node whose symbol is required. So that's covered.\n\nAlso, we need to ensure that after removal, the tree remains valid? Grammar-valid? The problem says \"grammar_guided_node_reduction\" but we are not given grammar rules. Actually the case only provides nodes with symbols. \"Grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates\" - but we are only doing deletion? The description says \"Reduce a registered parse tree through grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates\". But the task seems to only delete nodes (tentatively remove). Replacement might be handled elsewhere. The fixture shows only removal attribute. So likely we just delete removable nodes subject to constraints.\n\nAlso need to ensure that we maintain the tree structure: after removal, the children of the removed node become children of its parent? The typical Perses algorithm removes a node and promotes its children to the parent. But the description says \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" It doesn't specify promoting children. However, \"node with retained children\" implies that if a node has retained children, you cannot remove it because you would lose those children. So promotion is not happening? Actually if you remove a node, its children would become children of its parent? But that might change the tree structure. But the problem statement says \"grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates\". Possibly we are only performing deletion of leaf nodes? But the depth shows nodes at depth 2 have children? Actually nodes have parent, so leaf nodes are those that are not parent of any node. In the fixture, node n4 has parent n2, n5 parent n3, n6 parent n1. So nodes at depth 2 may have no children (they are leaves). So we can remove leaves. Then after removing a leaf, its parent may become a leaf? But then can remove parent later. That is typical bottom-up. So we should treat removal as simply removing the node from the retained set, and its parent will no longer have that child. But we must not remove a node if it has children that are still retained, because that would leave children without a parent. So we need to track retained children per node. So algorithm:\n\n- Build tree: map parent -> list of children.\n- Initialize retained set = set of all node IDs.\n- Build a map node_id -> node info.\n- Create a list of nodes sorted by depth descending, then by id ascending (string compare? Since IDs are like \"n1\", \"n2\", we can compare lexicographically, but ensure stable tie break. The problem says \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\" So we can use (depth, id) for sorting, descending depth, ascending id.\n- Iterate over sorted nodes. For each node, if its id not in retained (already removed) skip. If not removable (node.removable == False) skip. If its symbol in required_symbols skip. If it has any children that are in retained, skip. Then tentatively try remove: check if after removal, all required symbols are still present. That means: for each required symbol, there exists at least one retained node (after removal) with that symbol. Since we are only removing this node, we just check if the node's symbol is required; but we already skipped required symbols. However, there could be other nodes with the same symbol (e.g., symbol \"s1\" appears multiple times). If we remove a node with symbol \"s1\" but there is another retained node with symbol \"s1\", then allowed. So we need to count occurrences of each required symbol in retained set. So before removal, count. If after removal, all required symbols have count >=1, then proceed. But note that we might have multiple nodes with same required symbol. So we need to ensure that we do not remove the last node of a required symbol. So compute current counts of symbols among retained nodes. For the candidate removal, if the node's symbol is in required_symbols and the count of that symbol is 1, then removal would make it 0, so disallowed. Actually if symbol is required and we haven't skipped it, that means the node is not required? Wait we already skip if node.symbol in required_symbols. So we won't attempt to remove a node whose symbol is in required_symbols. But consider a required symbol like \"s1\" that appears in multiple nodes. The nodes with that symbol are not necessarily marked as required; the required_symbols list indicates that the overall parse tree must contain at least one node with that symbol after reduction. So we must not remove all nodes with that symbol. So we should allow removal of a node with symbol that is in required_symbols, if there is another retained node with that symbol. The instruction \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" The phrase \"a required symbol\" could mean \"a node whose symbol is in the required list\"? Or \"the root is a required symbol\"? Actually it says \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" It likely means: never remove the root (root node), never remove a node that is a required symbol (i.e., the symbol itself, not necessarily the occurrence). But the required_symbols list is a list of symbols that must be present. It's ambiguous. However, the example fixture: root symbol is \"root\", and required_symbols includes \"root\" and \"s1\". There are multiple nodes with symbol \"s1\" (n1 and n4 in first case). So if we remove node n4 (symbol s1), is that allowed? Possibly yes because n1 also has symbol s1. But if we remove n1, only n4 remains, then remove n4? That would remove all s1. So we must prevent the last s1 from being removed. The instruction says \"a required symbol\" likely means a node that is the only occurrence? Or the symbol itself? I think it's safer to interpret that a node whose symbol is in required_symbols cannot be removed if it would leave that symbol absent. But the phrasing \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" could be read as: never remove the root, never remove a node that is a required symbol (i.e., that node's symbol is required), and never remove a node with retained children. But that would mean if a node's symbol is in required_symbols, it can never be removed, even if there are duplicates. That might be too strict. The typical Perses algorithm uses \"required symbols\" to mean the set of symbols that must appear in the final output. So you can remove a node with a required symbol as long as there is another node with that symbol. However, the test cases: in the first case, nodes with symbol \"s1\" are n1 (depth 1) and n4 (depth 2). n1 is not removable (removable=false), n4 is removable. So it's likely intended that n4 can be removed because n1 still has s1. The instruction \"Never remove ... a required symbol\" probably means \"never remove a node that is the only occurrence of a required symbol\" but that's not what it says. Given the ambiguity, but the example suggests that required_symbols list is used to ensure the final set contains at least one of each. The algorithm must check that after each tentative removal, all required symbols remain present. So we should not hard-block removal of nodes with required symbols; instead we compute counts and allow if count >1. That matches typical reduction.\n\nGiven the \"hard contracts\" mention \"interestingness_preserved\" which is about satisfying some oracle, but we don't have oracle here; we just have required symbols. So we treat \"interestingness\" as satisfied if all required symbols are present. So we implement that.\n\nThus algorithm:\n\n- Build dict node_by_id.\n- Build children list per parent.\n- retained = set(all node ids)\n- symbol_counts = Counter(symbol for each retained node)\n- removal_order = []\n- sorted_nodes = sorted(node_by_id.values(), key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\n- for node in sorted_nodes:\n    if node['id'] not in retained: continue\n    if not node['removable']: continue\n    # check children: any child id in retained?\n    children = children_map.get(node['id'], [])\n    if any(cid in retained for cid in children): continue\n    # check if removal would drop required symbol count to zero\n    sym = node['symbol']\n    if sym in required_symbols and symbol_counts[sym] == 1: continue\n    # remove\n    retained.remove(node['id'])\n    symbol_counts[sym] -= 1\n    removal_order.append(node['id'])\n- After loop, sorted_retained = sorted(retained) (since IDs are strings, natural sort)\n- Return {\"retained_ids\": sorted_retained, \"removal_order\": removal_order}\n\nBut need to ensure that we reprocess nodes after removals? Since we process in descending depth, and we only check children currently retained, if a child is removed later, its parent might become removable. But in descending depth, parents have lower depth, so they are processed after children? Actually descending depth means deeper first. So children (depth 2) processed before parents (depth 1). So when processing parent, its children might have been removed already. So that's correct. However, we are iterating a fixed list, not re-sorting after removals. That's fine because we only attempt removal once per node.\n\nEdge: after removal, the parent's children retained set changes, but we already processed parent later, so we will see that children list may be empty or not. So it works.\n\nBut note: The requirement \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes.\" and \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\" That's what we do.\n\nAlso \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" So we return both.\n\nImplement as solve(case) that takes case dict with 'payload' containing 'nodes' and 'required_symbols'. Return dict.\n\nWe need to validate required fields: nodes, required_symbols. Raise ValueError if missing or infeasible? Infeasible could be if a required symbol not present initially? But that might be invalid case. The problem says \"Raise ValueError for an infeasible or structurally invalid case instead of silently repairing it.\" So we should check that required_symbols are all present in the initial set (at least one node with that symbol). Also check that nodes have required fields: id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility. And that root exists with parent null. Also that no cycles? Probably assume valid.\n\nWe'll implement basic validation: \n- Ensure 'nodes' is list, each has those keys.\n- Build map, ensure root exists.\n- Ensure all required_symbols appear in at least one node's symbol.\n- Raise ValueError otherwise.\n\nWe also need to ensure that the input is not mutated; we only read.\n\nNow write Python source as a string. Escape for JSON.\n\nWe'll produce:\n\n{\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    ...\\n\"}\n\nMake sure to only use standard library: collections for Counter? We can use collections.Counter. Or manually count. That's fine.\n\nWrite code with proper indentation.\n\nAlso note: The input case might have more than just nodes and required_symbols; there's _case_nonce but we ignore.\n\nNow produce output."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":1852,"completion_tokens":3761,"total_tokens":5613,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":1792},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":3062},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":1792,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":60},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}